# A tour of beam.com

## About this notebook

`beam.com` is Erlang/OTP and Elixir in one executable file. The same file runs on Linux, macOS, Windows, FreeBSD, NetBSD and OpenBSD. It also has a second runtime: the Erlang runtime system (ERTS) compiled to WebAssembly. `beam.com INPUT -o DIR --target wasm32` makes one directory that runs a program on Cloudflare Workers, on Deno Deploy, or in a web page.

This Livebook is such a program. The release of Livebook runs in that WebAssembly runtime, and each cell runs in the same VM as Livebook (the embedded runtime).

This notebook shows the parts of the BEAM that make it different: many small processes, messages, supervisors that start processes again, and code that changes while it runs.

Run the cells in order. To run all the cells, press <kbd>Esc</kbd>, then <kbd>e</kbd> and <kbd>a</kbd>.

## Where this runs

The runtime tells the program its host in the variable `BEAM_HOST`.

```elixir
host = System.get_env("BEAM_HOST", "native")

where =
  case host do
    "browser" -> "in this tab of your browser: no server runs this code"
    "cloudflare" -> "in a Cloudflare Worker (a Durable Object)"
    "deno-deploy" -> "on Deno Deploy"
    "deno" -> "in Deno"
    _ -> "natively, not in the WebAssembly runtime"
  end

{up, _} = :erlang.statistics(:wall_clock)

%{
  where: where,
  system: to_string(:erlang.system_info(:system_architecture)),
  otp: to_string(:erlang.system_info(:otp_release)),
  elixir: System.version(),
  schedulers: :erlang.system_info(:schedulers),
  word_size: :erlang.system_info(:wordsize),
  processes: length(Process.list()),
  uptime_seconds: div(up, 1000)
}
```

The system is `wasm32-unknown-emscripten`: the ERTS of a server, compiled to WebAssembly. The word size is 4 bytes, so a process takes about half the memory of a process on a 64-bit server. The VM has one scheduler: see the notebook "Inside the WebAssembly VM".

### A measure that needs no clock

On Cloudflare, the clock of a Worker moves only at I/O (a protection against timing attacks). There, `:timer.tc/1` gives 0 for code that does no I/O. The reductions of the VM count its units of work, and they need no clock. `Tour.measure/1` gives both.

```elixir
defmodule Tour do
  @doc "The time (ms) and the reductions of fun, and its result."
  def measure(fun) do
    {r0, _} = :erlang.statistics(:exact_reductions)
    t0 = System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
    result = fun.()
    t1 = System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)
    {r1, _} = :erlang.statistics(:exact_reductions)
    {%{ms: Float.round((t1 - t0) / 1_000, 1), reductions: r1 - r0}, result}
  end
end

Tour.measure(fn -> Enum.reduce(1..1_000_000, 0, &+/2) end)
```

Code that a cell evaluates runs in the interpreter of Erlang terms. The code of a module (such as `Enum`) is compiled BEAM code, so it takes fewer reductions.

## Many small processes

A BEAM process is not a thread of the operating system. It has its own small heap, and the VM switches between processes itself. Change the number, and run the next cell again.

```elixir
count_input = Kino.Input.number("Processes", default: 10_000)
```

```elixir
count = Kino.Input.read(count_input)

{cost, pids} =
  Tour.measure(fn ->
    for _ <- 1..count do
      spawn(fn ->
        receive do
          :stop -> :ok
        end
      end)
    end
  end)

bytes =
  pids
  |> Enum.map(fn pid -> Process.info(pid, :memory) end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {:memory, m} -> m end)
  |> Enum.sum()

Enum.each(pids, &send(&1, :stop))

"#{count} processes in #{cost.ms} ms (#{cost.reductions} reductions), #{div(bytes, count)} bytes each"
```

Livebook also runs Erlang cells. This is the same test in Erlang, with 1,000 processes. An Erlang cell sees the variables of the Elixir cells, with a capital letter (`Count` is `count`), so its own variables have other names:

```erlang
Procs = [spawn(fun() -> receive stop -> ok end end) || _ <- lists:seq(1, 1000)],
Memory = lists:sum([M || P <- Procs, {memory, M} <- [erlang:process_info(P, memory)]]),
[P ! stop || P <- Procs],
#{processes => length(Procs), bytes_each => Memory div length(Procs), elixir_count => Count}.
```

## Messages

Processes share no memory. They send messages. This ring sends one message around 1,000 processes, 100 times: 100,000 messages.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Ring do
  def start(size) do
    Enum.reduce(1..size, self(), fn _, next ->
      spawn(fn -> relay(next) end)
    end)
  end

  defp relay(next) do
    receive do
      :stop ->
        send(next, :stop)

      n ->
        send(next, n)
        relay(next)
    end
  end

  def run(first, rounds) do
    for n <- 1..rounds do
      send(first, n)

      receive do
        ^n -> :ok
      end
    end

    send(first, :stop)

    receive do
      :stop -> :ok
    end
  end
end

first = Tour.Ring.start(1_000)
{cost, :ok} = Tour.measure(fn -> Tour.Ring.run(first, 100) end)
"100,000 messages in #{cost.ms} ms (#{cost.reductions} reductions)"
```

Kino can draw the messages between processes. This cell traces one request and its reply:

```elixir
Kino.Process.render_seq_trace(fn ->
  parent = self()

  server =
    spawn(fn ->
      receive do
        {:ping, from} -> send(from, :pong)
      end
    end)

  send(server, {:ping, parent})

  receive do
    :pong -> :pong
  end
end)
```

## Let it crash

A supervisor starts processes and starts them again when they stop. The code of a process does not need to handle each error: it stops, and its supervisor starts a new one from a known state.

```elixir
children =
  for n <- 1..3 do
    Supervisor.child_spec({Agent, fn -> 0 end}, id: :"counter_#{n}")
  end

{:ok, sup} =
  Kino.start_child(%{
    id: :tour_supervisor,
    type: :supervisor,
    start: {Supervisor, :start_link, [children, [strategy: :one_for_one]]}
  })

Kino.Process.render_sup_tree(sup)
```

Now stop one of the counters. Look at the process ids before and after:

```elixir
before = for {id, pid, _, _} <- Supervisor.which_children(sup), do: {id, pid}
{id, victim} = Enum.random(before)
ref = Process.monitor(victim)
Process.exit(victim, :kill)

# Wait for the end of the process. Then the supervisor has the new counter.
receive do
  {:DOWN, ^ref, :process, _, _} -> :ok
end

now = for {id, pid, _, _} <- Supervisor.which_children(sup), do: {id, pid}

%{stopped: {id, victim}, before: before, after: now}
```

## Change the code while it runs

The BEAM can load a new version of a module while processes run the old one. A process moves to the new version at its next call through the module name (`__MODULE__.loop/1`). A release upgrade uses the same mechanism.

```elixir
defmodule Tour.Greeter do
  def loop(n) do
    receive do
      {:hello, from} ->
        send(from, {:reply, "version 1, call #{n}"})
        __MODULE__.loop(n + 1)
    end
  end
end

greeter = spawn(fn -> Tour.Greeter.loop(1) end)

ask = fn ->
  send(greeter, {:hello, self()})

  receive do
    {:reply, reply} -> reply
  end
end

{greeter, ask.(), ask.()}
```

This cell compiles version 2 of the module, and asks the same process (the same process id) two times:

* The first answer comes from version 1: the process waits in the `receive` of the old code.
* Its call to `__MODULE__.loop/1` goes to version 2, so the second answer comes from the new code.
* The process keeps its state (the call count) through the change.

```elixir
Code.compile_string("""
defmodule Tour.Greeter do
  def loop(n) do
    receive do
      {:hello, from} ->
        send(from, {:reply, "version 2, call \#{n}"})
        __MODULE__.loop(n + 1)
    end
  end
end
""")

{greeter, Process.alive?(greeter), ask.(), ask.()}
```

## A live view of the VM

Kino updates the output of a cell while the cell runs. This table shows the 10 processes with the most work (reductions), and it changes each second.

```elixir
frame = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()

Kino.listen(1_000, fn _ ->
  rows =
    Process.list()
    |> Enum.map(fn pid ->
      {pid, Process.info(pid, [:registered_name, :reductions, :memory, :message_queue_len])}
    end)
    |> Enum.reject(fn {_, info} -> info == nil end)
    |> Enum.sort_by(fn {_, info} -> info[:reductions] end, :desc)
    |> Enum.take(10)
    |> Enum.map(fn {pid, info} ->
      %{
        process: inspect(pid),
        name: if(info[:registered_name] == [], do: "", else: inspect(info[:registered_name])),
        reductions: info[:reductions],
        memory: info[:memory],
        queue: info[:message_queue_len]
      }
    end)

  Kino.Frame.render(
    frame,
    Kino.DataTable.new(rows,
      name: "The busiest processes",
      keys: [:process, :name, :reductions, :memory, :queue]
    )
  )
end)

Kino.nothing()
```

